FINDINGS
  - The decomposed kernels keep scaling to all 16 physical cores:
    P7 117.2 -> 8.16 s (14.4x at np16), A1/C1 16.6 -> 1.26 s (13.2x),
    P3 9.07 -> 1.12 s (8.1x, Allgather-limited). C1 behaves on Linux exactly
    as designed.
  - END-TO-END SWEET SPOT AT N=5e4 IS np8 (3.2-3.4x); np16 is net-NEGATIVE
    on this box. Cause: the REPLICATED memory-bound phases slow down as more
    ranks run the same O(N) sweeps concurrently and share DRAM bandwidth --
    A2-A1 residue 8.7 -> 15.4 -> 24.2 -> 52.1 s (np1->8->16->32), P2 and P4
    grow the same way. --bind-to core changes nothing (np16bind == np16), so
    it is bandwidth/cache contention, not rank migration. The M4 Pro's much
    higher per-core memory bandwidth masked this tax; per-core compute here
    is also ~2.6x slower than M4 Pro (np1: 164.7 vs 62.8 s).
  - This IS the re-ranking the strategy doc deferred C2/C3 for: on a
    bandwidth-limited host the next wins are the replicated predict /
    corrector / ADJUST-bookkeeping sweeps (P2+P4+P9+A2residue = 22 s of the
    np16 55 s SUM) and OUTPUT (9 s file I/O), not further O(N^2) splitting.
    The GPU-build re-measurement (G2) remains the decision point.
